    "body": "Thanks for working on this. I think `1c27203d1dc5` needs changes before merge because there is a real correctness issue at the signed minimum boundary.\n\n- `av_abs_i()` is not correct for the most negative representable `AVInteger` (`0x8000...0000`). `neg_i()` leaves that value unchanged in two's-complement arithmetic, so `av_abs_i()` can return a still-negative value even though the API says it returns `|a|`.\n- More importantly, `av_idiv_i()` then passes that result to `av_div_i()`. In `av_mod_i()`, a negative dividend is handled by recursing with `0 - a`; for the minimum value, `0 - a == a`, so this never makes progress and can recurse indefinitely / overflow the stack.\n\nA concrete reproducer would be something along the lines of dividing the 128-bit minimum value by `1` after constructing it via a left shift.\n\nCould you please either define and handle the unrepresentable case explicitly (for example with an assert/special-case/documented behavior), or otherwise avoid sending that value through the negative-dividend recursion path? A regression test for that boundary case would also be very helpful.",
